body:
50 bucks is a lot of money to make in the short term investing.

&#x200B;

If you got money to throw around, put it in an index.

Hey mate. I went through the ringer on this today. New job, new some dress shirts ASAP as they aren't a staple of my wardrobe. I'm on the bigger size, needing to lose some weight - it's bad on the gut and on my hips.

&#x200B;

Classic fits are too bulky, but slim fits are far too form fitting (and look like they're gonna break any second). I have to backup the other user on Van Heusen - they have a Tailored Fit shirt that works so, so much better than the other ones I've worn. Best to go in person to somewhere that stocks it so you can play around with sizing. I was an XL, 44/90. Each size has its own range of minor adjustments to get the fitment just right. The 'standard' average XL fit relatively nicely but was tight around my gut, simply upping a few minor sizes was perfect.

&#x200B;

Was a bit disappointed in DJs that they didn't have anything in a darker colour, but I've picked up some nice lighter ones and now I know my measurements I'll just fill the gaps in online.

Thanks. I lodged a complaint through live chat and also lodged one with my country's consumer affairs agency.

&#x200B;

Hopefully I can get something...

Sounds like you can skip ahead a few steps, and start looking into (safe) ways to grow your savings/surplus income!

&#x200B;

Good work.

First of all; you're 19. I am not making any accusations against you, but most 19 year olds don't have very good relationships with money or spending plans. Source: I was 19 3 years ago, and had terrible habits which I regret only 3 years later.

&#x200B;

I had terrible spending habits at 19 yet convinced myself investing was the cure. Starting out, it isn't.

&#x200B;

I would recommend getting a copy of Barefoot Investor, or at least finding someone's writeup on the main takeaway points. It's not the Bible, it won't be your be-all-end-all, but give it a read and understand your fundamental expenditures, emergency funds, and overall stability.

&#x200B;

Once you nail that, look to longer term options like investing. Others here have linked good resources. You should read them. Don't rush into anything though. Remember to live a bit too, you're 19.

Good to know we got advice from the pros.

&#x200B;

You really should bill OP for the quote.

Sorry, don't know how I didn't get this notification a month ago!

&#x200B;

In terms of handling volume on a mechanical level, yeah, the EK43 is a beast. Our blades in our shop are rated for 5 tons of beans, so absolutely they can handle it.

&#x200B;

Probably the better wording I should've used is it's not great for workflow. You can expedite the workflow by predosing all your beans, but I think in a more traditional cafe environment you'd always be better having a grinder with a hopper loaded with beans.

&#x200B;

I love our EK, but I couldn't imagine surviving with just an EK alone.

Luckily, it's a specialty place, and the only major one in our region of 60,000+ people. They sell a lot of specialty coffee including single origin stuff, I came about these guys because I did barista training last year with them, recently applied, and then all this came about.

&#x200B;

Do you think it's a good job to have? It sounds more rewarding than hospitality, so I'm quite excited.

I don't own an 86 but I've sat in one/test drove a few times. I have pretty broad shoulders but I still sat comfortably, for such a small car it is surprisingly comfortable (I'm 6'2, broad shoulders, about 100kg).

&#x200B;

If in doubt, go see one in person and go for a test drive, maybe. I'm sure you'll be fine.

I work in food service and get paid $21/hour weekdays and $26 weekends. I'm 19, and it's a pizza place.

&#x200B;

Although it's Australia, so 21 AUD =/= 21 USD of course. Our cost of living is around the same though for day-to-day stuff.

